            "Microsoft Research describes TokenEconomizer as an open-source plugin from UC Berkeley, Microsoft Research and Microsoft Security, and says that 'Across 277 real bug-fixing tasks, TokenEconomizer reduced costs by 18.4–30.9% across multiple AI coding agents', evaluated on 277 SWE-Bench tasks from six repositories using five agent and model combinations.",
            "Profiling of Claude Code on 60 SWE-Bench tasks found that 30% of tool calls repeated an action from an earlier task in that repository, that the agent received 23 tool descriptions on every turn but 16 were never called, and that the 17% of tasks that failed accounted for 32% of the spending.",
            "The post reports further savings of 16.0–21.2% on Web-Bench and 6.4–19.6% on security auditing, says Pass@1 rose in four of the five combinations, and says reported savings account for TokenEconomizer's own model calls.",
            "These are Microsoft's own measurements and have not been independently reproduced in the sources seen here."
          ],
          "topics": ["microsoft", "agents", "compute"],
          "impact": "beneficial",
          "flags": ["company-claim"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "items": [
        {
          "headline": "Google: threat actors automated vulnerability discovery and exploitation with no human in the loop for the first time",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "Google Threat Intelligence Group", "url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/agentic-threat-activity-crosses-a-significant-threshold/" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "GTIG writes: 'In the past quarter, GTIG identified threat actors using LLM frameworks to fully automate vulnerability discovery and exploitation without a human in the loop for the first time.' It found an exposed staging server hosting a multi-agent framework it dubs exploit_agent, integrating multiple frontier and local LLMs, which 'compares code before and after patch announcements, analyzes findings to identify potential vulnerabilities, drafts exploits, and automatically executes them without human intervention'.",
