        { "host": "A", "text": "It adds licensing for five more countries. Malaysia, Singapore, Indonesia, Thailand and Brazil, which Commerce said present a higher-than-normal risk of diversion. Existing operators have 90 days from the rule taking effect to get licences." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "It also rescinds the AI Diffusion Rule from January 2025. Singapore's trade and digital ministries said they were studying the details of this new measure carefully, and engaging affected companies." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "One honest limitation. We could not open the rule document itself. The agency's press pages returned errors and the Federal Register blocked us, so everything there comes through TechCrunch and a law firm's alert, not the rule text." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Military, defense & geopolitics",
      "headline": "CSIS: India's CERT-In logged its first AI-agent cyber incident and now requires six-hour reporting",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "CSIS says that on October 15th, India's emergency response team disclosed its first cyber incident where an AI agent, not a human operator, was responsible. It was rated critical severity." },
