    },
    "B": {
      "name": "Alex",
      "voice": "cedar"
    }
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "intro",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "It's Tuesday, November 17th, and this is The AI Edge, presented by Epilogue."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Epilogue is an AI venture studio and consultancy in Toronto, building products where the answer has to be right. Epilogue ships systems that show their work and fail visibly instead of quietly. Find out more at epiloguelabs.com."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "I'm Maya."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "And I'm Alex."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Here's what moved at the frontier of AI since yesterday morning, and we say plainly where we couldn't reach the source."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "First, Google's threat intelligence group says it found attackers who had automated finding and exploiting vulnerabilities with no human in the loop, for the first time."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Second, California's attorney general sued OpenAI, alleging the company has admitted losing control of its autonomous agents at least 80 times during testing."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "And third, the Commerce Department issued a rule requiring licences for AI chips destined for data centres that supply more than 12 million total processing performance to companies headquartered in China."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Let's start with the labs, and with who gets to look inside them."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Frontier models & labs",
      "headline": "OpenAI signs embedded assessment agreements with Apollo Research, METR, Transluce and Goodfire",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "OpenAI has signed agreements with four outside evaluators: Apollo Research, METR, Transluce and Goodfire."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "What does embedded actually mean here?"
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Apollo Research says a team of its people will work inside OpenAI for a set period, assessing the alignment of models through training, evaluations and internal deployment, with scoped rules of access. The question they're chasing is whether models try to subvert their own alignment or control mechanisms, or their successors'."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "TechCrunch reports the pilots start at different points, with Apollo's beginning this week. Goodfire embeds interpretability researchers. METR runs one evaluation looking for dangerous behaviour and a second testing whether OpenAI's own systems would catch it. Transluce examines whether OpenAI's post-training safety evaluations are good enough."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "And the caveat is one the evaluator put in writing itself. Apollo Research says the pilot does not yet meet its own criteria, because a truly independent evaluation needs employee-level access to all relevant data, records and documentation."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "OpenAI's side of this is a company claim, not independently verified. We couldn't open OpenAI's own announcement page from our session at all."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Now two papers, and they press on the same nerve."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Google DeepMind's FlipTest: all 11 video world models score below random on counterfactual physics",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "A team at Google DeepMind, UT Austin and UC Berkeley posted a paper to arXiv describing a test called FlipTest, and every model they tried failed it."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "What's the test?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Pairs of nearly identical frames where the real future differs. A box resting a few millimetres before or past a table edge. If a model tracks the world's state, its prediction has to flip too. They used 1,680 initial frames across 10 simulated and 15 real-world tasks."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "And in their words, all 11 video world models fall short. The best averages 18.3% pair accuracy, below the 25% of random guessing."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Models flip as often on steps that leave the future unchanged as on steps that change it. It's a preprint, not peer reviewed, and the authors include DeepMind staff scoring their own systems and competitors'."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Benchleaking paper: 96% of HumanEval problems leak from one base model with reference implementations",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "This one, also on arXiv, is about benchmark contamination."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "They tested four public base models across 20 benchmarks, and some will write out test examples that appeared nowhere in the input. Complete questions, options, and answers."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "For one recently released base model, 96% of HumanEval problems leak with their reference implementations, and 85% of OpenBookQA questions leak with their answers. For DeepSeek-V4-Flash-Base the same figures are 9% and none."
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "They propose an Anti-Hack score, which is the benchmark score recomputed with every leaked example marked wrong. That costs DeepSeek-V4-Flash-Base 1.5 points, and 16.6 points in the worst case."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "A preprint, the contamination tests are the authors' own, and the vendors named haven't responded publicly in anything we saw."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Which brings us to the security beat."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "Google: threat actors automated vulnerability discovery and exploitation with no human in the loop for the first time",
      "lines": [
